#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060686} :activity/id #uuid "ccb8f334-2616-5c6c-bf9f-ac7f7a6bbbf5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049422} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060686 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060941 :tutorial/content "Consider the following system of linear equations and its respective **augmented matrix**:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+6y &=-2 \\\\ 3x-y &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n$$\nM =\n\t\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{array}\n\t\\right]\n$$\n\nIf we can convert the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form, then we can solve the system using back substitution.\n\nThe procedure for reducing $M$ to row echelon form using elementary row operations is called **Gaussian elimination**. To perform Gaussian elimination on a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, we use the following procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** Consider the entry $a_{11}=2$ of the coefficient matrix. This entry is called a **pivot element**. We want to make this entry equal to $1$. So, the first elementary row operation will be \n\n$$\nR_1 := \\dfrac{1}{2}R_1\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Consider the entry $a_{21}=3$ below the first pivot element. We want to make this entry equal to $0$. So, the second elementary row operation will be \n\n$$\nR_2 :=R_2+(-3)R_1\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}{\\color{red}1} & 3 & -1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}3} & -1 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+({\\color{blue}-3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}{\\color{red}1} & 3 & -1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & -10 & 5\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, consider the entry $a_{22}=-10$. This entry is the second pivot element, and we would like to make it equal to $1$. So, the third elementary row operation will be \n\n$$\nR_2 :=\\dfrac{1}{-10}R_2\n$$\n\nand we get $M$ in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & {\\color{red}-10} & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{{\\color{red}-10}}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & {\\color{red}1} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+3y &=-1 \\\\ y &=-\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+3 \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) = -1 \n\t\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\tx=\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $y=-\\dfrac{1}{2}.$ Or, in vector form, $\\left< \\dfrac{1}{2}, -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right>.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2112d3af-1fb0-5a70-ab99-59931db99930" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3151 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4af04e11-a9c8-594e-af53-b43d73f0e12d" :step/math-academy-id 17188 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060688}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060687 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059775 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059584 :question/id #uuid "0a9e6824-677a-5fae-9867-1701e7d8378f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4697" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\n  \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -8\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y &=0 \\\\ y &=1,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=1$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+2(1)=0 \n\t\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\n\tx=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-2$ and $y=1.$ Or, in vector form, $\\left<  -2,1 \\right>.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a10cafc6-9da7-5ffc-a0c7-686a7638fbd9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050527}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674013} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186640023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674010}] :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a 2x2 Augmented Matrix to REF Using Gaussian Elimination"} :step/id #uuid "2b9b40ab-3cb7-53b3-bb37-414d8e65412f" :step/math-academy-id 17189 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060689}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060688 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060942 :tutorial/content "The row echelon form of a matrix is *not* unique. In the previous example, after the first row operation we arrived at \n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}{\\color{red}1} & 3 & -1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{red}-10} & 5\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nThis augmented matrix is already in row echelon form! However, it is always useful to do an extra step and obtain $1$'s along the main diagonal, like we did previously:\n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}{\\color{red}1} & 3 & -1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{red}1} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nHaving $1$'s along the main diagonal makes the final back substitution process a little easier." :tutorial/id #uuid "ee4b134d-2908-5930-86bb-32537e0d8347" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3152 :tutorial/title "The Uniqueness of the Row Echelon Form Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "bafcaf30-9671-5655-95bd-e04dfa8750ef" :step/math-academy-id 17190 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060687}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060689 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059776 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059585 :question/id #uuid "1eea81be-26d7-57dd-a622-d66300de7f4f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4698" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x+5y &=5 \\\\ x-6y &=5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\n\t\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -6 & 5\\end{array}\n\t\\right].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -6 & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -5 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -6 & 5\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -5 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 10\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -5 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -10\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-5y &=-5 \\\\ y &=-10,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=-10$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx-5(-10)=-5 \n\t\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\n\tx=-55.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-55$ and $y=-10.$ Or, in vector form, $\\langle -55,-10 \\rangle.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07abc1cc-5794-51a6-bad4-40565eae52be" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050765}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528050} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414018}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 2x2 System of Equations Using Gaussian Elimination"} :step/id #uuid "381e4e89-5395-58a9-bdf6-3f814147b17f" :step/math-academy-id 17191}] :activity/title "Solving 2x2 Systems of Equations Using Gaussian Elimination" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059884}]}
